Dear Dave,



I have finished my review of your design. I hope that it is not too late. A=
s I wrote in my last E-mail, it looks a very nice and useful board. I am re=
ally looking forward to it.



Since almost all components you used are new for me, it took a long time to=
 dig through all the documentation, but that is done now. Please, find atta=
ched my review report, your latest schematic with my issues highlighted on =
it and the BOM that you made but now with (Farnell) order numbers and so on=
. I also flagged a view issues in this BOM, like two similar components whe=
re when capacitor is made with X7R and the other with X5R dielectric, or so=
mething similar. I am not sure that I have incorporated all the suggestions=
 in the BOM also in my word file, so it might be worth reading my BOM file.



As I mentioned in my previous E-mail, I flag quite a few (sometimes trivial=
) issues. Thank you very much for your positive reply to my previous E-mail=
 about the number of issues/professional pride. Most of my issues are just =
suggestions and I am happy if you ignore them. I pose a lot of questions in=
 the report, but you don't have to answer them in writing; writing takes a =
lot of time. These questions are more things for me to remember and which I=
 can ask you verbally at some stage.



Thank you very much again and I hope that my review will be useful to you.



Greetings,



Erik Schooneveld



________________________________
From: David Cussans [David.Cussans@bristol.ac.uk]
Sent: 04 June 2014 10:22
To: Schooneveld, Erik (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Cc: Sykora, Jeffrey (STFC,RAL,ISIS); Rhodes, Nigel (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Subject: Re: ISIS wish list

Erik,

    Rather later than I had hoped for, but I finally managed to get a coupl=
e of clear weeks to design the single MAROC board.

    I am just sending the board out for quotes for component placement and =
routing.

    I have attached a copy of the circuit schematic, a slightly updated ver=
sion of the pin-out of the two DIN41612 expansion connectors, the netlist, =
the BoM and a sketch of the probable layout.

    I have also attached a copy of the PCB database with partial component =
placement in Cadence Allegro ( *.brd)  format ( in case you can read this f=
ormat or are able to download the free reader from Cadence )

    If you are able to find any time to check to see if this will do what y=
ou want ( and to see if you can spot any errors ) I would be very grateful.

    I still need to dig out the cross-talk figures for you. One of my colle=
agues has the data on disk, but he is in Germany at the moment. From what I=
 recall cross-talk with our existing boards is a couple of percent for adja=
cent channels.

    Perhaps more critical for your application is the minimum time between =
pulses. This isn't important to us with cosmic ray muons, but I had a quick=
 look at the performance of the threshold discriminators a few days ago thi=
nking of you. We can get up to about 3MHz of pulses without tuning ( the un=
i-polar and bi-polar fast shapers have different performances ). What is th=
e minimum time you expect between your PMT pulses? If we fiddle a bit we ca=
n probably get the MAROC up to 10MHz ( i.e. pulses < 100ns apart are merged=
 into one ) but I doubt it would go much further than that. Is this adequat=
e?

    Cheers,
            David

On 04/04/14 19:04, erik.schooneveld@stfc.ac.uk<mailto:erik.schooneveld@stfc=
.ac.uk> wrote:
Dear David,

Thank you very much for your E-mail. No problem at all that you haven=92t c=
ontacted us before, we know all about how long it takes to make a design, h=
eavy work load/priorities of various projects and I can understand that thi=
s project isn=92t your highest priority one. I was recently considering to =
contact you and ask for the status/progress because in order to start the d=
esign of our own plug in cards we need to know the  whether the pin connect=
ions  of the 96-pin expansion connectors of your board have changed during =
the lay-out of the PCB. With a draft of your schematic in a few weeks=92 ti=
me we will know. With a draft lay-out, I can also start thinking about the =
interface board between our PMTs and your MAROC.

For sure, I am very happy to have a look at your draft schematic and layout=
 when it is finished. I can imagine that it helps you finding bugs and it w=
ill also help us getting the board in operation quicker; better to spot err=
ors before the production of the board than after it has been produced. As =
you say, it would also give us the opportunity to give our input on the imp=
lementation of our wishes; I can guarantee you that I will not be too picky=
.

The only thing I want to make clear is that obviously I haven=92t made a PC=
B for the MAROC chip before, so I cannot comment for example on (for exampl=
e) cross talk issues of the digital signals on the analogue signals or simi=
lar kind of things. You are the expert and have made MAROC boards before, s=
o I am sure you will do it correctly and don=92t need my advice for those i=
ssues. Likewise, I haven=92t made the layout for fast serial communication =
lines, so I have no idea about how acceptable/robust compromises in the lay=
out of the serial lines; all I can do is give my best guesses without being=
 able to speak from experience.

Summarizing: For sure, I am very pleased to help you as best as I can with =
the schematic and layout of your MAROC board.

Thanks again for making such a very useful MAROC board and giving us the op=
portunity to give our wishes for the features.

Greetings,

Erik Schooneveld


From: David Cussans [mailto:David.Cussans@bristol.ac.uk]
Sent: 04 April 2014 16:39
To: Schooneveld, Erik (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Cc: Sykora, Jeffrey (STFC,RAL,ISIS); Rhodes, Nigel (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Subject: Re: ISIS wish list

Erik, Jeff,
    My apologies that I have been quiet for so long. I should have a draft =
schematic and hopefully a draft layout in the next few weeks.

    Is there any chance you could have a look at it when it is finished? Th=
is would help me find any bugs and also make sure that I have understood wh=
at features would be useful to you.

         Many Thanks,
                David


On 14/02/14 13:26, erik.schooneveld@stfc.ac.uk<mailto:erik.schooneveld@stfc=
.ac.uk> wrote:
Dear David,

That is splendid news, you have been able to accommodate a lot of our wishe=
s. Thank you very much.

No problem with a =93late=94 reply. It is the same here, with urgent jobs p=
opping up. Thinking about an E-mail and writing a reply takes a lot of time=
. I am grateful that you have found the time to have a serious look at our =
wishes, rather than skimming the document and say yes, yes. Your reply/read=
ing of the document is even more thorough than I had envisaged.

For sure, I thank you very much for increasing the number of signal lines o=
n the expansion connectors, making it for our purposes a very versatile boa=
rd. I hope that my wish list hasn=92t forced you to using more =93low=94 sp=
eed expansion connector signals if you preferred fewer high speed communica=
tion lines. A male connector on one side and female connector on the other =
side is perfectly OK for us; we can use different genders on our expansion =
boards.

Replies to your comments:
Scenario 1) You raise a very good point about coupling/cross-talk between s=
ignals in a differential pair; I hadn=92t thought about that. Since all sig=
nals are clock synchronized on the expansion connectors, so I hope/think th=
at our 33MHz signals are slow enough to be able to handle the cross-talk. A=
t least they are slower than the 50-80MHz limit you estimate. Thinking abou=
t this issue, I realized that we might actually be better off when we use 3=
2-pairs of differential signals and do a double data-rate trick to read out=
 64 channels. Nevertheless, I think that I will start with reading out 32 c=
hannels with differential LVDS, which fit straight into our discriminator e=
lectronics, and still have a very useful fraction of the PMT available for =
the evaluation. Later I can investigate how to read out the full 64 channel=
s.
Scenarios 2+3) Great that we will be able to do those.
Scenario 4) Thank you very much for your offer to find an IP-bus expert for=
 us if we are interested.
Scenario 5) Thank you very much for the explanation and the link. I hope I =
will be able to find a copper and/or optical SFP unit that fits our require=
ments. I think that I will try to use one Bristol board as the sender and a=
nother board as the receiver in which case I can use (practically) the same=
 firmware as used for scenario 3. I am curious to see how many channels we =
can combine onto a single link.

Wish 3.1.1) Thanks a lot, a large FPGA will make it a more versatile board =
for us.
Wish 3.1.2) Thank you very much for the spreadsheet. Your plans look perfec=
t for us.
Wish 3.1.3) Great that you have a lot of grounds, that will help us both fo=
r differential and single ended signals.
Wish 3.1.4) A 200ps jitter is no problem at all for us. I had problems with=
 some Altera boards that the delays are longer/varying more from compilatio=
n to compilation when clock outputs are taken from general user IO pins, ra=
ther than dedicated clock output pins. But even in those cases, I didn=92t =
have problems running a 160MHz clock with a fixed delay on the PLL, so for =
it will be OK for the slower clock/data rate on this board. Anyway, Xilinx =
FPGAs could have less delays when using clock outputs on normal IO pins. I =
appreciate that you have a clock input and output per connector, making it =
possible to have 2 independent (own) IO boards on the expansion connectors.
Wish 3.1.5) 12V power supplies are no problem for us. I like the fact that =
each connector has a 12V =93input=94 and =93output=94 so we can supply the =
power to the Bristol board through the expansion connector; keeps a nice an=
d tidy setup. I agree that DC-DC converters are small nowadays, so it is ea=
sy to make any voltage out of a 12V input. I have been interested in the sm=
all size Murata converters, like the Murata LXDC55K, for a while and this p=
roject is a good =93excuse=94 to use them. Our own expansion boards don=92t=
 have sensitive analogue signals, so bit of noise shouldn=92t be a problem;=
 we have a DAC but that is just used for diagnostics/monitoring so noise on=
 that signal is not affecting our data.
Wish 3.1.6) No I certainly wasn=92t thinking about giga-bit transceivers; s=
uch a fast signals are restricted to the SFP. I was thinking about either v=
ery slow control signals, like starting or stopping a run, or ~100Mbit/s si=
gnals. These ~100Mbit/s signals can be used for example when one MAROC is g=
iving the x-position of the event and another MAROC the y-position, in whic=
h case we have to send the x and y-positions to some kind of combiner board=
 to get the x,y position. Since we don=92t need to know the timing of the e=
vent to better than 100ns-1us, we don=92t need giga-bit data lines. As your=
 normal data lines (D0-D15) can go up to ~160MHz we might use these lines, =
the only thing is that I don=92t know whether on Xilinx FPGAs you can conne=
ct a SerDes to any differential IO line or only to dedicated GTP pins.
Wish 3.1.7) That is great.
Wish 3.1.8) Super. It looks like the SFP will be a nice and flexible connec=
tor that we can (misuse) for anything we like.
Wish 3.1.9) It is absolutely not my intention to try and convince you to pu=
t a Kintex-7 (or Artix-7) on your boards and give you extra work and expens=
es. You should do what is most convenient for you. I just wanted to write i=
t down because I was surprised that a Kintex-7 was so cheap; the 160K versi=
on is half the price of an Altera Cyclone III (Spartan 3 equivalent) with 1=
20K logic units. Who knows, if we are making Xilinx boards in the future th=
e Kintex family would be my first choice. As I wrote in my document, this w=
ish is just pie-in-the-sky that you can forget (but obviously would be very=
 nice if it fits with your future needs).

Thank you very much again for your reply and your willingness to help us; y=
our design incorporates a lot of our wishes and will be perfect for our eva=
luation.

We (including Jaap and Dave), will need a discussion later about how we can=
 proceed with the purchase of your boards.

All the best,

Erik Schooneveld


From: David Cussans [mailto:David.Cussans@bristol.ac.uk]
Sent: 13 February 2014 17:45
To: Schooneveld, Erik (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Cc: Sykora, Jeffrey (STFC,RAL,ISIS); Rhodes, Nigel (STFC,RAL,ISIS)
Subject: Re: ISIS wish list

Erik,

    Many apologies for my slow reply - I had to do some "fire fighting" on =
another project, also I wanted to read through your document rather than sa=
ying "yes, of course, my board will do everything ...." .

    As far as I understand it, I think all five scenarios outlined in your =
document "ISIS wish list for the Bristol MAROC evaluation board".

    At the moment I plan to use DIN 41612 96-way connectors for the two exp=
ansion connectors. These aren't "high tech" but they are cheap, robust and =
easy to obtain. Following from this I am now aiming at 160MBit/s on 16 pair=
s on each connector, rather than 320MBit/s per pair on 8pairs. Each expansi=
on connector will also have a data-clock line. Since the data and clock lin=
es will be directly connected to the FPGA they could also be configured as =
34 single-ended signals per connector ( A total of 68 single-ended signals =
for the board ).
    I am keeping my aim of being able to stack up to five of these boards s=
ide-by-side, with read-out from the end board ( there is one male and one f=
emale right-angled connector on each board ).
    To make things a bit clearer I have attached a possible pin-out for the=
 expansion connectors.

    Comments/caveats on scenarios:

Scenario 1) You should have enough signal lines if the signals on the expan=
sion connectors are configured as single-ended. However, the PCB traces wil=
l be routed as differential pairs, so you would need to reduce the edge-spe=
ed from the FPGA to avoid excessive cross-talk. My guess is that up to 50-8=
0MBit/s per pin would be fine given the short distances. You  might even li=
ke to consider a single-ended standard with termination e.g. SSTL_2 if you =
want high rates.

Scenario 2,3) Should be no problem.

Scenario 4) There is starting to be a reasonably large "user community" for=
 IPBus. If the MAROC works satisfactorily for your detectors I should be ab=
le to find an IPBus expert who could talk to you about how IPBus might fit =
into your DAQ.

Scenario 5) The SFP cage will be directly connected to one of the "GTP" Gig=
abit transceivers in the Spartan-6. These aren't general purpose pins - the=
y are dedicated to the high-speed transceiver. However,  there is no protoc=
ol hard-wired into the transceiver: You push parallel data into the seriali=
zer and pull parallel data out of the deserializer. Features like 8B/10B en=
coding, comma alignment, clock correction are optional ( if I understand th=
e manual correctly. ). See Xilinx UG386 , http://www.xilinx.com/support/doc=
umentation/user_guides/ug386.pdf. Minimum, maximum data rate =3D 614MBit/s =
, 3.125 GBit/s .


Comments on wish list:

3.1.1: The xc6slx150t is pin compatible with the xc6slx25t. If I design wit=
h the '25t all we have to do is have boards assembled with the '150t for yo=
u.

3.1.2: Compatible with my current plan. See spread-sheet.

3.1.3: I have a lot of ground pins to give me the best chance possible at h=
igh data-rate on the DIN 41612 connectors.

3.1.4 One pair of pins on each connector will go to pins on the FPGa that c=
an be used for clock input.  Any general purpose I/O can be used for clock =
output (including the clock inputs). Jitter when driving as a clock output =
is not that good - normally about 200ps pk-pk. Would this be OK?

3.1.5 To supply enough power I was planning to use 12V and DC-DC converters=
 ( and lots of filtering to keep the noise away from the MAROC ). This isn'=
t 3.3V, but small DC-DC converters are easy to get hold of and use ( unless=
 you want to put them close to low noise electronics ..... )

3.1.6 Do you mean putting connecting up giga-bit transceivers to the expans=
ion connectors? This might be a bit of a challenge if I stick with DIN 4161=
2 connectors.

3.1.7 I need to put mounting-holes on the PCB to accommodate the daughter b=
oards that I will be designing. I'll let you have an initial placement as s=
oon as I have it.

3.1.8 Yes, the SFP will be connected directly to GTP pins on FPGA.

3.1.9 I don't rule out moving to a Kintex-7 in a later model, but I only ha=
ve direct experience of working with the Spartan-6, so to get things done i=
n time I think the first boards (at least) will have a Spartan-6

        Cheers,
        David


General comments:

    I have decided to implement a clocking system compatible with the CERN =
"White-Rabbit" implementation of PTP ( IEEE-1588 ). This should allow the c=
locks inside a distributed collection of FPGAs to be aligned to better than=
 1ns.


We have enough MAROC chips in hand to make you two or three boards. I sugge=
st that we make you perhaps two boards with xc6slx150t for initial tests an=
d then make more when you are happy with them.

Your time-scale looks compatible with my project plan.

The circuit schematic and PCB layout will be done in Cadence Allegro using =
the CERN symbol/footprint library. I hope to release the design files under=
 the "Open Hardware Licence" ( see ohwr.org ) but need to check with the IP=
 people at Bristol first.

        Cheers,
            David








On 10/02/14 17:27, erik.schooneveld@stfc.ac.uk<mailto:erik.schooneveld@stfc=
.ac.uk> wrote:
Dear David,

Have you been able to find some time to give the ISIS wish list that I sent=
 ~2 weeks ago a bit of thought?
I am curious to know whether you find some of my wishes realistic to implem=
ent on your board. Let me make clear that I don=92t want to bury you into m=
ore work or make your board unnecessarily complicated/time consuming to dev=
elop. I just wrote down everything I could think of, so that later I don=92=
t regret not mentioning it.

You don=92t have to read the first two chapters if you are short on time, a=
s they are mainly background information and a record of our plans with Mar=
oc chips. I left it in the document because it can give you an idea why the=
 items are in our wish list and you might be interested in our plans with t=
he MAROC chip.

Looking forward to your opinion about our wish list, but above all to the B=
ristol board with MAROC chip.

Regards,

Erik Schooneveld


